      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's clear up the fog and dig into the correct morphological analysis of the words you've provided.\n\n1. talossani: The root is indeed \"talo,\" which means \"house\" in Finnish. The suffix \"-ssa\" indicates the inessive case, which is used to denote being inside something. So, \"talossa\" translates to \"in the house.\" The \"-ni\" suffix indicates the first person singular possessive, so \"talossani\" means \"in my house.\" There are no vowel harmony or consonant gradation changes in this word.\n\n2. kaupassamme: The root is \"kauppa,\" meaning \"trade\" or \"business.\" The suffix \"-mme\" indicates the first person plural possessive, so \"kaupassamme\" translates to \"our trade\" or \"our business.\" There are no vowel harmony or consonant gradation changes in this word either.\n\n3. kynsiäni: The root is \"kynsi,\" which indeed means \"nail\" in Finnish. The suffix \"-äni\" indicates the first person singular possessive, so \"kynsiäni\" means \"my nail.\" There are no vowel harmony or consonant gradation changes in this",
